"Vorg the Sapient" <spam@nospam.com> wrote:

> It's always heavily reliant on luck.

One of the hardest things to learn, and I'm nowhere
close to there yet, is simply not to choose to do
things where the outcome depends on luck, at all,
when any other choice is possible.

Rog-o-matic showed the way: it was careful to step
back into its own footprints whenever possible.

A NetHack player can make the choice to step only in
a place already proved almost surely trap free by a
pet or another monster stepping there first, can
focus obsessively on staying out of ranged weapon
lines of sight from monsters who might be instadeath
wielders.

Don't drink unIDed potions, don't read unIDed
scrolls, don't wear unPetStomped armor, or armor
that might be dangerous even when uncursed, don't
read unblessed spellbooks, don't kick sinks, don't
drink from fountains, check whether a monster is
peaceful and thus no threat before attacking it,
don't sit on thrones, don't fight monsters you can
outrun, don't fight monsters your pet can kill for
you, don't eat corpses you didn't kill yourself
or see killed right before your eyes one turn ago,
don't pray if the maximum possible prayer timeout
hasn't expired, when you see a corpse upon entering
a room, stop to worry about what, on that very spot,
might have killed it, kick grey stones, open doors
from beside, not from in front of them, fight from
restricted areas to minimize simultaneous melee, ...

There are likely to be hundreds of other places
similar tricks can remove the "luck" component
either entirely or almost so.

I'm dying with huge piles and stashes of untried
"stuff" these days, but I'm dying a _lot_ further
into the game on average.

NetHack is _so_ much about minimizing risk, I'd bet
actuaries would make the best players of all.

xanthian, still rushing in where Purrball fears
to tred, but trying to learn better habits.



